  "body": " Check Your Understanding    Which file normally contains the Rectangle class definition and its member-function declarations?     Rectangle.h   The header provides the class definition and declarations needed by source files that use Rectangle .     Rectangle.cpp   The implementation file normally contains the member-function definitions.     main.cpp    main.cpp can use the class by including its header, but the class definition belongs in the header in this organization.        Which directive is the usual way to include a header that belongs to your own project?     #include <Rectangle.h>   Angle brackets are normally used for standard-library and other installed library headers.     #include \"Rectangle.h\"   Quotation marks are the usual form for a header that belongs to the current project.     #include Rectangle.h   The header name must be enclosed in quotation marks or angle brackets.        What is the main purpose of a header guard?      It prevents other source files from using the class.    A header is intended to be shared by source files that need its declarations.      It prevents the protected header contents from being processed repeatedly when the same header is encountered more than once while compiling a source file.    The guard allows the header contents to be processed once for that compilation and skipped on later encounters.      It automatically makes all class data private.    Access control is determined by public: , private: , and the type's default access rules.        Arrange the blocks to create the basic protected contents of Robot.h .     #ifndef ROBOT_H_INCLUDED    #define ROBOT_H_INCLUDED    class Robot {  public:  Robot();  private:  int battery_level;  };    #endif    #include ROBOT_H_INCLUDED    #ifndef \"Robot.h\"       A file named Book.h contains this declaration:  class Book { public: Book(const std::string& initial_title); private: std::string title; };  Which standard-library header should Book.h include itself?     <iostream>   The declarations shown do not use stream types or std::cout .     <string>   The header directly uses std::string , so it should include <string> itself.     No header is needed.   A header should include the declarations required by the types it uses rather than depending on another file to include them first.      "
